.TH VENIX 4 "2026-10-08" "Substrate" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
venix \- Venix/86 (Version 7 UNIX for the IBM PC) execution personality
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B Venix
personality
.RB ( PERS_VENIX )
runs the programs of VenturCom Venix/86 2.1, a port of Version 7 UNIX to
the 8086 and 8088.
It is rooted at
.IR /perso/venix ,
where a program's absolute paths are looked for first.
.PP
System III numbered its system calls as Version 7 did and added to them,
and Xenix/286 inherited the result; the structures the calls fill in did
not change on the way.
A Venix call is therefore run by the Xenix/286 implementation of it, as
a PC/IX call is
.RB ( pcix (4)).
What follows was read from the C library on the distribution.
.SS Executable format
.B struct exec
of Venix's
.IR <a.out.h> ,
32 bytes: the magic number, the size of the stack, then the sizes of
text, data, bss and symbols, the entry point and the relocation sizes.
.TP
.B 0407
Text and data in one segment.
.TP
.B 0411
A segment each.
.PP
The stack is at the bottom of the data segment and not the top:
.B a_stack
bytes, growing down towards 0, with the data (in an 0407 file the text
and then the data) above it.
The break may rise to the end of the segment.
An
.B a_stack
of 0 puts the stack at the top instead.
On entry the stack pointer addresses
.IR argc ,
followed by the argument pointers, a null, the environment pointers and
a null.
.PP
Magic 0411 is no other system's.
0407 is every a.out's, and such a file is taken for a Venix program when
a Venix process executes it or it is named under
.IR /perso/venix .
.SS System calls
Every call is the instruction
.BR "int 0xf1" ,
with its number in BX and its arguments in AX, DX, CX and SI.
The result is in AX and a second result, or the high half of a long, in
DX.
CX is the error number, 0 if there was none; the carry flag means
nothing.
.PP
The calls are Version 7's by number.
.B fork
returns 0 in the child.
.B brk
succeeds for an address between the end of the program and the end of
its segment (or the stack, when that is at the top).
.B stat
and
.B fstat
report the type of a file in
.B st_mode
as the Sixth Edition did, which Venix kept: 0100000 for a file, 0140000
a directory, 0120000 and 0160000 the special files.
.B ioctl
has the terminal requests
.BR TIOCGETP ,
.B TIOCSETP
and
.B TIOCSETN
on a six-byte
.BR "struct sgttyb" .
.SS Venix's own calls
The distribution documents these only as argument lists in its lint
library; what they do was read from its kernel.
.TP
.BR semset "(n, pri), " semclear "(n), " semtest "(n), " semtset "(n, pri)"
Semaphores, each one bit.
.B semset
waits until
.I n
is clear and sets it;
.B semtset
sets it if it is clear and returns 1 if it was already set;
.B semtest
returns whether it is set;
.B semclear
clears it and wakes the waiters.
A negative
.I n
names one of sixteen semaphores the whole system shares, \-1 the first;
0 to 15 name sixteen shared by the processes running the same program.
A semaphore left set by a process that exits stays set.
.TP
.BR sdata "(path, r, 0)"
Attaches the file
.I path
as a segment of data shared with every process that attaches it, and
points the ES register at it.
What is stored there is stored in the file.
.BR sdata "(1, r, n)"
makes
.IR n *512
bytes of shared data with no name, for the process and the children it
then has;
.BR sdata "(0, r, n)"
moves ES
.IR n *512
bytes into what is attached;
.BR sdata "(2, r, 0)"
detaches it, and ES is the data segment again.
A segment is at most 64K.
.TP
.BR phys "(r, s, a)"
Points ES at physical address
.IR a *512.
Only the adapter and ROM area, 0xA0000 up to 1 MB, may be asked for
.RB ( EPERM
otherwise, and while shared data is attached); Venix allowed its
superuser all of memory, which here is not a PC's.
.TP
.BR suspend "(pid, flag)"
Stops the process, or lets it run again if
.I flag
is 0.
.TP
.BR lock "(flag)"
Keeps the process in memory.
It always is; the call succeeds, for the superuser only when
.I flag
is not 0.
.TP
.BR locking "(fd, mode, size)"
Locks
.I size
bytes of the file from the descriptor's position, or to its end if
.I size
is 0.
Mode 0 unlocks, 1 locks or fails with
.B EACCES
if another process holds any of the range, 2 waits for it.
The locks are between processes on the file, thirty in the system as in
Venix, and are apart from
.BR fcntl (2)
record locks.
A process's locks go when it exits, not when it closes the file.
.TP
.BR aiowait "(fd, flag)"
Returns 0: no asynchronous transfer is ever outstanding.
.PP
A program reaches the segment ES is pointed at with an ES: prefix; the
C compiler has no notation for that.
After
.BR fork (2)
the child's ES points where the parent's does, at the same shared data;
a mapping made by
.B phys
is not inherited.
.SS Other traps
.TP
.B int 0xf4
Precedes each 8087 instruction, for a kernel without a coprocessor to
emulate it by.
The two bytes are overwritten with no-ops the first time each is
reached and the instruction runs on the processor.
.TP
.B int 0xf2
The stack check at the start of a function failed; the process gets
.BR SIGSEGV .
.TP
.B int 0xf3
.BR abort (3);
the process gets
.BR SIGABRT .
.SS Signals
A handler is entered with the flags and the interrupted instruction
pointer pushed, the flags on top, and every register as it was.
It returns with
.B popf
and a near
.BR ret .
The disposition is reset to the default first and nothing is blocked.
The signal number is not passed: the C library registers a stub of its
own for each signal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /perso/venix
The Venix tree.
.I tools/venix/build-image.sh
builds an image of it from floppy images.
.SH NOTES
Enable tracing of the calls with
.B debug=perso:venix:syscall
on the kernel command line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pcix (4),
.BR svr3 (4)
